From 05af132852b7fa830648cd61a9dd63e09cd537c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:59:32 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] Signals: report RF in the EFLAGS of a fault's signal
 frame

What x86-64 Linux does: for a fault-class exception (#UD, #GP, #PF, ...) the CPU pushes an EFLAGS image with RF
(resume flag, bit 16) set, and the kernel copies that image into uc_mcontext.gregs[REG_EFL] unchanged. A SIGSEGV
from a page fault, a SIGILL from ud2, a SIGSEGV from a privileged instruction all show RF = 1. Trap-class exceptions
(#BP from int3, #DB, #OF) and asynchronous signals show RF = 0. rt_sigreturn accepts RF back (FIX_EFLAGS); the CPU
clears it after the next instruction, PUSHF never stores it and POPF clears it.

What FEX did: the running RF is always 0 and the frame EFLAGS is the reconstructed running EFLAGS, so a fault frame
had RF = 0. Who observes it: Wine copies the frame EFLAGS into CONTEXT.EFlags, so every PE exception handler sees
it. A title's own execute-fault handler (THE FINALS, during thread detach) gets EFlags 0x10202 on x86-64 Proton and
0x00000202 under FEX; it is the only flag difference in those exception records.

Fix:
* SignalDelegator: when the guest frame is built for a fault (a generated exception whose trap number is not
  #DB/#BP/#OF, or a host SIGSEGV/SIGBUS on a guest access), set RF in the frame EFLAGS. One branch per guest signal
  delivery; nothing on any other path.
* RF never stays in the running state: SetFlagsFromCompactedEFLAGS (rt_sigreturn, Wine context loads) and
  SetPackedRFLAG (POPF, IRET) store 0. An IRET that loads RF only masks an instruction breakpoint on the next
  instruction, and FEX has no instruction breakpoints, so this is exact for everything a guest can see; without
  it Wine's `pushq %r11; popfq` return path would carry RF = 1 into PUSHF and later frames.

Test: eac-tests/fault-eflags (frame EFLAGS = flags before the instruction | RF for #PF read/exec, #UD, #GP;
RF clear for int3; all 16 GPRs and RIP in the frame, also for an execute fault right after a syscall; PUSHF after
the handler returns shows RF clear).

Upstream: FEX main as triaged at 14c9268 builds the frame from the running EFLAGS (RF 0) and stores RF from POPF/IRET.
#DE is not covered: FEX does not raise it for a division by zero (upstream limit, no consumer seen).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
