From 9b618d2e1feb12f2461481c781a4d970735b4e2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 00:18:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] ArchHelpers: set PF_USER on every guest page fault

GetProtectFlags (arm64) gated X86_PF_USER on the ESR data-abort walk-level
field being EL0 (0b11). That field records which page-table level resolved
the abort, not which exception level the access came from; guest accesses
always fault at EL0. Faults that resolved at walk level 0-2 (first delivery
of a fault in a large unmapped gap) therefore lost PF_USER and the guest saw
err=0x0 instead of the native 0x4/0x6/0x14/0x15 (PF_USER is bit 2, set by the
kernel on every user-mode page fault). PF_USER is now unconditional; the
walk-level constants are left for GetESRName only.

Verified with tests/alt-nested (check 1 err 0x0 -> 0x4, matching the native
oracle) and tests/segv-regs case 5 (MAPERR / err 0x14). Fault path only; zero
cost when no fault is delivered.

The second change this patch originally carried -- ForceGuestSigSegv
unblocking SIGSEGV before raising it -- duplicated the audit fix to patch
0019, where that code was introduced. That fix is in the series now, so this
patch is reduced to the GetProtectFlags change and rebased onto it.

Base: FEX-2610 14c92681 + patches 0001-0027.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/ArchHelpers/MContext.h | 11 +++++++----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/ArchHelpers/MContext.h b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/ArchHelpers/MContext.h
index fb6ab8c..aaa11c1 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/ArchHelpers/MContext.h
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/ArchHelpers/MContext.h
@@ -232,10 +232,13 @@ static inline uint32_t GetProtectFlags(void* ucontext) {
                      ESR1_EC_DataAbort, GetESRName(ESR));
 
   uint32_t ProtectFlags {};
-  if ((ESR & ESR1_DataAbort_Level) == ESR1_DataAbort_Level_EL0) {
-    // Always a user error for us.
-    ProtectFlags |= FEXCore::X86State::X86_PF_USER;
-  }
+
+  // A guest code memory access always faults at EL0, so PF_USER is always set: the kernel sets it for every
+  // user-mode page fault (bit 2 of the x86 error code, present in every reported value: 0x4 read, 0x6 write,
+  // 0x14/0x15 instruction fetch). The ESR walk-level field says which page-table level resolved, not which
+  // exception level the access came from, so it must not gate PF_USER: faults resolving at walk level 0-2
+  // (e.g. a fetch from an address in an unmapped huge gap) lost PF_USER and reported err=0x0.
+  ProtectFlags |= FEXCore::X86State::X86_PF_USER;
 
   if (ESR & ESR1_WNR) {
     // Fault was due to a write
