From 40a9803f0d920d3613a0e256b7ce970c5fb38d70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:11:49 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] JIT: validate the fetched bytes of an undecodable
 instruction on SMC-checked pages

An instruction the decoder rejects has InstSize 0. On a page that is checked for self-modifying code (every page with
FEX_SMCCHECKS=full, and the hot pages patch 0002 leaves writable) the translator emitted a ValidateCode for it over zero
bytes. The JIT never writes its CRC register for a zero-length check, so the comparison reported "code changed" on every
execution; the block removed its own entry and re-translated, and the thread looped in translation forever (millions of
"Invalid instruction in entry block" log lines, 100% CPU, the guest never sees the signal).

Validating a fixed single byte instead is not enough: an instruction rewritten in place that shares that byte (the
rejected `lldt ax`, 0F 00 D0, followed by `ltr ax`, 0F 00 D8, or `swapgs`, 0F 01 F8) passes the check and reuses the
cached #UD block instead of raising its own #GP. The decoder now records how many leading bytes it actually fetched (read
or peeked, never skipped or substituted) before rejecting the instruction, in DecodedInst::RejectedSize, and the core
validates exactly those bytes. A rejection with nothing fetched is an instruction fetch fault, which does not depend on
the contents, so no check is emitted for it (this also avoids reading an inaccessible byte at translation time).

Reproduced without Wine: tests/invalid-ops phase 2 (one code page rewritten and executed repeatedly, the pattern of
Wine's exception tests and of runtime code generators) hung at the first undecodable instruction after the page turned
hot; with a one-byte check it no longer hangs but reports ltr/swapgs as #UD; phase 3 alternates the rejected and the
valid encodings on one page. Native x86-64 Linux is the oracle (#GP, trapno 13, for ltr and swapgs).

Consumer: any program that executes an undecodable instruction from a page it also modifies (Wine/Proton exception
tests, runtime code generators); the symptom is a permanent hang, or the wrong exception class. Cost: one byte compare
per fetched byte at decode time; the emitted check is the one already emitted for every instruction on such pages.

Upstream: Core.cpp ValidateCode and Frontend.cpp DecodeInstruction on FEX main (checked 2026-10-11) are unchanged, so
FEX_SMCCHECKS=full is affected there as well; patch 0002 is what makes the default configuration reach it.

