From 9e8e99fb655719188a6e4d3bbee11c9c3764583d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] JIT: report the page boundary as si_addr for an
 instruction fetch that straddles into a non-executable page

When an instruction starts on an executable page and its remaining bytes lie on a page without execute
permission, x86 raises #PF for the first byte it cannot fetch: CR2, and so the SIGSEGV si_addr the kernel
reports (arch/x86/mm/fault.c, force_sig_fault(SIGSEGV, si_code, address)), is the start of the second page,
while RIP stays at the instruction start. FEX raised the fetch fault through NoExecOp, whose generated
SIGSEGV always used the instruction start (OriginalRIP) as si_addr, so the guest was told the wrong page.

Consumer: Wine turns the signal into EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION with ExceptionInformation[0] = 8 (execute)
and ExceptionInformation[1] = si_addr (dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c, segv_handler). A handler that
makes the page at ExceptionInformation[1] executable (lazily committed or protected code pages) and
continues fixes the page that is already executable; the same fault repeats forever.

The decoder already knows the address: CheckRangeExecutable walks executable ranges and stops on the
first byte no range covers. That address is kept for the instruction being decoded, turned into an
offset from the instruction start (ranges are page granular, so it is the faulting page's base) and
carried in the Break definition (FaultAddressOffset, one byte taken from the padding of
SynchronousFaultData). The signal frame builder adds it to OriginalRIP. Every other generated fault
keeps offset 0 and is unchanged; the cost is one byte in a constant that is already stored on the fault
path, nothing on the translation or execution path of valid code.

Test: tests/segv-regs case 7 (mov $imm32 two bytes before the end of an r-x page, next page rw-):
expected (kernel source) si_addr = page boundary, RIP = instruction start; FEX before this change gives
si_addr = RIP (source reading). Case 8 (run a page, drop PROT_EXEC, run it again) guards the invalidation
side; GuestMprotect invalidates the range, so it is expected to pass before and after.
NOT YET COMPILED OR RUN: native and FEX results pending.

Upstream FEX main has the same NoExecOp and the same OriginalRIP si_addr (checked in the pinned tree;
re-check main before carrying this). The Windows frontends (Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h) build
their own EXCEPTION_RECORD and are not changed here.

Base: FEX-2610 14c92681 + patches 0001-0026, 0030-0032 (applies independently of 0027-0029 and 0033;
with 0027, UnmappedOp should carry the same offset).

