From e98364e74493bf549e34ed5644e89c8c1d16e672 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aqua <cps@Aquas-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 07:26:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] OpcodeDispatcher: raise #DE for DIV/IDIV with a zero
 divisor or an overflowing quotient

What x86-64 Linux does: DIV and IDIV raise #DE when the divisor is zero and when the quotient does not fit the
destination (DIV: upper half of the dividend >= divisor; IDIV: e.g. INT_MIN / -1, or a 128-bit dividend whose
quotient needs more than 64 bits). The kernel delivers it as SIGFPE with si_code FPE_INTDIV and trapno 0 at the
divide instruction, registers unchanged (arch/x86/kernel/traps.c exc_divide_error); Wine turns it into
EXCEPTION_INT_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO for structured exception handlers.

What FEX did: Arm64 divides never trap, so a zero divisor gave a zero quotient, an overflowing one was truncated,
and execution carried on. Upstream lists this as a known failure (FEX-Emu/FEX#1980, closed in favour of the
Known_Failures lists); FEX main still has no #DE. Who observes it: any program whose control flow goes through the
exception, typically SEH-based code that divides by a computed zero on purpose (obfuscated control flow, self
checks), plus the exception record a debugger-aware runtime inspects. Found by eac-tests/insn-diff, the
differential instruction test against a native x86-64 oracle: 1263 of 2400 random DIV/IDIV inputs faulted natively
and none did under FEX.

Fix: the frontend branches to a block that raises SIGFPE/FPE_INTDIV/#DE at the divide. All checks use plain ALU
ops and cbz/cbnz/tbz (the guest NZCV is never touched) and every IR value stays inside its block:
  DIV: before the divide, tbz on the borrow of RDX - divisor (unsigned; AH for 8-bit): #DE unless RDX < divisor,
  which also covers a zero divisor. The operands are re-read after the branch (free for registers, which live in
  host registers; a memory divisor is read a second time).
  IDIV: before the divide, cbz on the divisor. Then, when the dividend is the sign extension of its low half (what
  CBW/CWD/CDQ/CQO produce, i.e. almost all compiled code), the only overflow is MIN / -1, checked from the inputs
  with a cbz that is off the critical path of the result. Any other dividend takes a slow path that checks the
  quotient itself: 8/16/32-bit divides are exact in the wider precision FEX already uses, so the quotient must
  sign-extend from the operand size; for 64-bit the 128/64 divide truncates, so Quotient * Divisor + Remainder must
  equal RDX:RAX (high halves compared). There RAX/RDX are blended with their old values under the resulting
  all-ones mask, so a faulting divide leaves them unchanged, and one cbnz at the end takes the fault.
DiskCache FormatVersion 30 -> 31 and the InstCountCI DIV/IDIV entries updated (generated code changes; the
pre-existing popf/pop ss count differences on this branch are left alone).

Cost, measured on Neoverse N2 (GitHub ubuntu-24.04-arm, eac-tests/insn-diff CI divbench, 50M dependent divides):
div32 4.33 -> 4.30 ns, div64 6.07 -> 5.96 ns, idiv32 4.88 -> 4.99 ns, idiv64 6.02 -> 6.34 ns. Nothing outside
DIV/IDIV changes.

The Linux frontend
delivers the break (which leaves the JIT through the SIGTRAP trampoline) with the guest's SIGFPE disposition; the
Windows frontend maps it to EXCEPTION_INT_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO like Wine does on Linux.

